// ================================================================ 3 PROBLEM
add(H1("3 Problem Formulation and Preliminaries"));
add(H2("3.1 Setting"));
add(P("We observe a stream (X_{1},Y_{1}), (X_{2},Y_{2}), … with X_{t} ∈ 𝒳 and Y_{t} ∈ ℝ. At each time t a forecaster f̂_{t}, trained only on data before t, produces a point prediction f̂_{t}(X_{t}); the forecaster may be refitted periodically. We use the absolute-residual nonconformity score s_{t} = |Y_{t} − f̂_{t}(X_{t})|. Before Y_{t} is revealed, an online conformal method outputs a threshold q_{t} computed from past scores and forms the interval"));
add(EQ("Ĉ_{t}(X_{t}) = [ f̂_{t}(X_{t}) − q_{t} ,  f̂_{t}(X_{t}) + q_{t} ],       err_{t} = 𝟙{ s_{t} > q_{t} }.", 1));
add(P("The target miscoverage is α ∈ (0,1). We model non-stationarity as a sequence of *regimes*: unknown change points 0 = τ_{0} < τ_{1} < … partition time such that, within a regime, scores are (approximately) exchangeable, while their distribution may differ arbitrarily across regimes and may recur."));
add(H2("3.2 Evaluation criteria"));
add(P("Long-run coverage (1/T)Σ(1 − err_{t}) is necessary but insufficient, since a method can over-cover in one regime and under-cover in another. Following earlier work (Angelopoulos et al., 2023; Gibbs & Candès, 2021, 2024), we also measure behaviour over time. With rolling coverage c̄_{t} computed over a window of w = 100 steps, we report: (i) marginal coverage; (ii) mean interval width 2q̄ (infinite thresholds, which ACI-type methods produce when α_{t} ≤ 0, are clipped at the largest observed score, and their frequency is reported separately as Inf%); (iii) local coverage error LCE = mean_{t}|c̄_{t} − (1 − α)|; (iv) under-coverage time UCT, the percentage of steps with c̄_{t} < 1 − α − 0.05; and (v) on synthetic streams with known change points, coverage over the 200 steps following each change (post-shift coverage) and its worst case across changes."));
add(H2("3.3 Split conformal quantile and ACI"));
add(P("For a calibration multiset 𝓑 of n scores and a level a, the conformal quantile is"));
add(EQ("Q(𝓑; a) = s_{(k)} with k = ⌈(n+1)(1 − a)⌉,   Q = +∞ if a ≤ 0 or k > n,   Q = 0 if a ≥ 1,", 2));
add(P("where s_{(k)} is the k-th smallest element. Rolling CP uses the last W scores as 𝓑. ACI (Gibbs & Candès, 2021) sets q_{t} = Q(𝓑_{t}; α_{t}) and updates"));
add(EQ("α_{t+1} = α_{t} + γ(α − err_{t}).", 3));
add(H2("3.4 Conformal test martingales"));
add(P("Given a multiset 𝓗 of n past scores, the smoothed conformal p-value of a new score s is"));
add(EQ("p(s; 𝓗) = ( #{h ∈ 𝓗 : h > s} + U · (#{h ∈ 𝓗 : h = s} + 1) ) / (n + 1),   U ~ Unif(0,1).", 4));
add(P("If the scores are exchangeable and each p-value is computed against all previous scores, the p-values are independent and uniformly distributed on [0,1] (Volkhonskiy et al., 2017; Vovk et al., 2005). A *betting function* g: [0,1] → [0,∞) with ∫g = 1 then yields a nonnegative martingale M_{t} = Π g(p_{j}) with M_{0} = 1, and Ville’s inequality (Ville, 1939) gives P(sup_{t} M_{t} ≥ 1/δ) ≤ δ. The simple-jumper martingale (Vovk, 2021; Vovk et al., 2021) mixes betting functions g_{ε}(p) = 1 + ε(p − 1/2), ε ∈ {−1, 0, 1}, allowing capital to ‘jump’ between them with rate J, which lets it detect changes that occur long after monitoring starts."));

// ================================================================ 4 METHOD
add(H1("4 The Proposed MARS-CP Framework"));
add(H2("4.1 Overview"));
add(P("MARS-CP (Fig. 1) runs three paths. The *prediction path* is an unmodified online CP updater (ACI, QT or DtACI) that computes q_{t} from the calibration buffer 𝓑_{k} of the current regime k. The *monitoring path* converts every new score into a conformal p-value with respect to the history of the current regime and feeds it to a two-sided test martingale (Algorithm 2). The *response path* (Algorithm 3) is executed only when the martingale raises an alarm: it localises the change point, closes regime k, opens regime k+1 with a buffer made of post-change scores, and re-initialises the updater. An optional regime-memory bank stores closed regimes and recalls a matching one when a regime recurs. Algorithm 1 combines the three paths, and Fig. 2 traces the life cycle of a regime through them."));
add(FIG(__dirname + "/../figures/fig1_framework.png", 3654, 2061, "**Fig. 1** Architecture of MARS-CP. The prediction path is any online conformal updater operating on the current regime buffer. The monitoring path computes regime-conditional conformal p-values and a two-sided simple-jumper test martingale. The response path, triggered only when the martingale exceeds 1/δ, localises the change, rebuilds the buffer and re-initialises the updater; the regime-memory bank is optional"));
add(H2("4.2 Regime-conditional conformal p-values"));
add(P("Let 𝓗_{k} denote all scores observed since regime k was opened (including the post-change scores it was initialised with). When s_{t} is revealed, MARS-CP computes p_{t} = p(s_{t}; 𝓗_{k}) by Eq. (4) and inserts s_{t} into 𝓗_{k}, kept sorted so that each p-value costs O(log n) comparisons. Crucially, p-values are computed against the *full* regime history rather than the size-W calibration window, which is what makes them independent and uniform when the regime is exchangeable, and hence what makes the false-alarm guarantee of Sect. 5 hold. The p-values do not depend on q_{t} or on the inner updater, so the monitor is identical for all wrapped methods."));
add(H2("4.3 Two-sided simple-jumper martingale"));
add(P("Small p-values mean that new scores are *larger* than those of the current regime (the stream became harder and coverage is threatened); large p-values mean that they are *smaller* (the stream became easier and intervals are needlessly wide). Coverage-driven updaters see the first kind of change through errors but see the second only weakly. MARS-CP therefore uses the full simple jumper with states ε ∈ {−1, 0, +1}: the state ε = −1 profits from small p-values, ε = +1 from large ones, and ε = 0 is neutral. With capital vector C_{t} over the states, each step performs"));
add(EQ("C_{t} ← (1 − J) C_{t−1} + (J/3) · ΣC_{t−1},     C_{t}(ε) ← C_{t}(ε) · (1 + ε(p_{t} − 1/2)),     M_{t} = Σ_{ε} C_{t}(ε),", 5));
add(P("computed in log-space for numerical stability. An alarm is raised when log M_{t} ≥ log(1/δ). Algorithm 2 gives the monitoring procedure, including change localisation. The one-sided variant (states {−1, 0}) is evaluated as an ablation."));
add(ALGO("Algorithm 2  MONITOR: two-sided jumper update and change localisation", [
  [0, "**Input:** new score s_{t}; sorted regime history 𝓗; capital vector C over ε ∈ {−1, 0, +1}; log-martingale path Λ; jumper rate J; budget δ."],
  [0, "**Output:** alarm flag and, on alarm, the localised change point ĉ."],
  [0, "n ← |𝓗|;  g ← #{h ∈ 𝓗 : h > s_{t}};  e ← #{h ∈ 𝓗 : h = s_{t}};  draw U ~ Unif(0,1)"],
  [0, "p_{t} ← (g + U(e + 1)) / (n + 1)                                ▷ regime-conditional p-value, Eq. (4)"],
  [0, "C ← (1 − J) C + (J/3) ΣC                                         ▷ jumping between betting states"],
  [0, "**for** ε ∈ {−1, 0, +1} **do**  C(ε) ← C(ε) · (1 + ε(p_{t} − 1/2))   ▷ ε = −1 bets on harder, ε = +1 on easier"],
  [0, "Λ_{t} ← Λ_{t−1} + log ΣC;  C ← C / ΣC                       ▷ log-space normalisation"],
  [0, "insert s_{t} into 𝓗 (sorted);  append Λ_{t} to path"],
  [0, "**if** Λ_{t} ≥ log(1/δ) **then**"],
  [1, "ĉ ← (index of the first monitored score) + argmin_{j} Λ_{j}     ▷ evidence started to grow after ĉ, Eq. (6)"],
  [1, "**return** (ALARM, ĉ)"],
  [0, "**return** (NO ALARM)"]]));
add(H2("4.4 Change localisation, regime reset and conservative bridge"));
add(P("A martingale alarm occurs after the change, with a delay that depends on its magnitude. Discarding only the scores after the alarm would waste post-change information, while keeping all scores since the last reset would contaminate the new buffer with pre-change data. MARS-CP localises the change at"));
add(EQ("ĉ = argmin_{j ≤ t} log M_{j},", 6));
add(P("the point after which the accumulated evidence started to grow, in the spirit of CUSUM (Page, 1954). Scores after ĉ (at most W of them) form the new buffer 𝓑_{k+1} and history 𝓗_{k+1}, the martingale is reset to M = 1, and the inner updater is re-initialised: ACI restarts from α_{t} = α; QT restarts from q_{t} = Q(𝓑_{k+1}; α); for DtACI, the experts’ levels restart from α while the *expert weights are retained*, because the step size that suited the previous regime is informative about the next. The pre-change scores are kept as 𝓑_{prev}."));
add(P("Immediately after a reset the new buffer may contain very few scores, and Eq. (2) would return +∞. For the first n_{min} scores of a new regime (and unless a recall has occurred), MARS-CP therefore uses the *conservative bridge*"));
add(EQ("q_{t} = max{ min(q^{inner}_{t}, max 𝓑_{k+1}),  Q(𝓑_{prev}; α) },", 7));
add(P("which never falls below the previous regime’s quantile while fresh evidence is scarce, and grows immediately if the new scores are larger. Figure 2 shows how these steps form the life cycle of a regime, and Algorithm 3 states the response procedure."));
add(FIG(__dirname + "/../figures/fig2_regime_lifecycle.png", 3560, 2260, "**Fig. 2** Life cycle of a regime in MARS-CP. In the steady state, every revealed score updates the regime-conditional p-value and the two-sided jumper. An alarm triggers change localisation, closes the regime, opens a new one from post-change scores, re-initialises the inner updater, applies the conservative bridge and, optionally, attempts a single recall from the regime-memory bank"));
add(ALGO("Algorithm 3  RESPOND: regime reset, updater re-initialisation and optional recall", [
  [0, "**Input:** localised change point ĉ; ordered regime scores S; recalled scores 𝓜 (possibly empty); inner updater 𝒰; window W; memory settings (n_{match}, ρ, B)."],
  [0, "S_{pre} ← scores of S before ĉ;  S_{post} ← last W scores of S from ĉ onward"],
  [0, "𝓑_{prev} ← last W scores of (𝓜 ⧺ S_{pre})                       ▷ previous regime, used by the bridge, Eq. (7)"],
  [0, "**if** memory **and** |𝓑_{prev}| ≥ n_{match} **then** push (𝓑_{prev}, state of 𝒰) to bank; keep the last B entries"],
  [0, "𝓑 ← S_{post};  𝓗 ← sort(S_{post});  C ← (1/3, 1/3, 1/3);  path ← [0];  𝓜 ← ∅"],
  [0, "re-initialise 𝒰:  ACI: α_{t} ← α;  QT: q_{t} ← Q(𝓑; α) (max 𝓑 if infinite);  DtACI: expert levels ← α, keep weights"],
  [0, "mark bridge active for the next n_{min} scores;  recall_tried ← false"],
  [0, "**procedure** RECALL (called once |𝓗| ≥ n_{match} and recall_tried = false)"],
  [1, "recall_tried ← true;  j′ ← argmax_{j} KS-p(𝓗, bank_{j})          ▷ two-sample Kolmogorov–Smirnov p-value"],
  [1, "**if** KS-p(𝓗, bank_{j′}) > ρ **then** 𝓜 ← scores of bank_{j′};  𝓑 ← 𝓜 ⧺ 𝓑;  restore 𝒰 state;  remove bank_{j′}"],
  [0, "▷ recalled scores enter 𝓑 but never 𝓗, so recall cannot affect false-alarm control"]]));
add(H2("4.5 Optional regime memory"));
add(P("Many streams revisit earlier regimes (weekday/weekend demand, machine operating modes). MARS-CP can store each closed regime (its last W scores and final updater level) in a bank of capacity B. Once the new regime holds n_{match} scores, a two-sample Kolmogorov–Smirnov test compares it with every banked regime; if the largest p-value exceeds a recall threshold ρ, the matched scores are prepended to the calibration buffer and the updater state is restored. Only one recall attempt is made per regime, so that the KS tests do not become a multiple-testing problem. Recalled scores enter the calibration buffer but *not* the martingale history, so recall cannot affect false-alarm control."));
add(H2("4.6 Complete procedure and complexity"));
add(P("Algorithm 1 gives the complete procedure, calling Algorithm 2 at every step and Algorithm 3 on every alarm. Per step, the p-value and insertion cost O(log n) comparisons plus an O(n) list insertion, the conformal quantile costs O(W) by selection, and the martingale update is O(1). The response path costs O(n) per alarm, and each recall attempt O(B·W log W). The measured overhead is below 0.1 ms per step for every inner updater (Sect. 7.7)."));
add(ALGO("Algorithm 1  MARS-CP (wrapping an inner updater 𝒰 ∈ {ACI, QT, DtACI})", [
  [0, "**Input:** target α; window W; false-alarm budget δ; jumper rate J; bridge length n_{min}; memory flag, n_{match}, ρ, B; initial scores s_{1}, …, s_{n₀}."],
  [0, "**Init:** 𝓑 ← last W initial scores; 𝓗 ← sorted initial scores; C ← (1/3, 1/3, 1/3); path ← [0]; bank ← ∅; 𝓑_{prev} ← ∅; initialise 𝒰."],
  [0, "**for** t = n₀+1, n₀+2, … **do**"],
  [1, "q_{t} ← 𝒰.threshold(𝓑)                                ▷ Eq. (2)/(3), QT or DtACI rule"],
  [1, "**if** bridge active **and** no recall in this regime **then** apply the conservative bridge, Eq. (7)"],
  [1, "output Ĉ_{t} = [ f̂_{t}(X_{t}) ± q_{t} ];  observe Y_{t};  s_{t} ← |Y_{t} − f̂_{t}(X_{t})|;  err_{t} ← 𝟙{s_{t} > q_{t}}"],
  [1, "𝒰.update(s_{t}, err_{t});  insert s_{t} into 𝓑 (keep the last W)"],
  [1, "(flag, ĉ) ← MONITOR(s_{t}, 𝓗, C, path)                           ▷ Algorithm 2"],
  [1, "**if** memory **and** |𝓗| ≥ n_{match} **and** bank ≠ ∅ **then** RECALL     ▷ Algorithm 3, at most once per regime"],
  [1, "**if** flag = ALARM **then** RESPOND(ĉ, regime scores, 𝒰)              ▷ Algorithm 3"],
  [0, "**end for**"]]));

// ================================================================ 5 THEORY
add(H1("5 Theoretical Properties"));
add(P("**Proposition 1 (Anytime false-alarm control).** Suppose that, from the opening of a regime onward, the scores are exchangeable and have a continuous distribution or ties are broken by the randomisation in Eq. (4). Then the probability that MARS-CP ever raises an alarm within that regime is at most δ, for any stopping rule and any horizon."));
add(P("*Proof.* Under exchangeability, smoothed conformal p-values computed against all previous scores in the sequence are independent and Unif(0,1) (Volkhonskiy et al., 2017; Vovk et al., 2005). MARS-CP computes p_{t} against the full regime history 𝓗_{k}, which includes the scores that opened the regime, so this condition holds. Each betting function g_{ε}(p) = 1 + ε(p − 1/2) with |ε| ≤ 1 is nonnegative and integrates to one on [0,1]. The mixing step in Eq. (5) redistributes capital without changing its total, so M_{t} = M_{t−1}·Σ_{ε}w_{t}(ε)g_{ε}(p_{t}) with predictable weights w_{t} summing to one. Hence E[M_{t} | past] = M_{t−1}, and M is a nonnegative martingale with M_{0} = 1. Ville’s inequality (Ville, 1939) gives P(∃t: M_{t} ≥ 1/δ) ≤ δ. □"));
add(P("**Corollary 1 (Conservativeness on exchangeable streams).** If the entire stream is exchangeable, then with probability at least 1 − δ MARS-CP never alarms, the memory bank remains empty, and its thresholds coincide with those of its inner updater run on the same size-W window."));
add(P("*Proof.* Without an alarm, no reset, bridge or recall is ever executed (recall requires a non-empty bank, which is filled only on alarms), so the prediction path is exactly the inner updater. The claim follows from Proposition 1. □"));
add(P("**Proposition 2 (Long-run coverage, ACI inner updater).** Let K_{T} be the number of re-initialisations of α_{t} (alarms plus recalls) up to time T, and assume that scores are almost surely positive. For any sequence of data, MARS-CP with the ACI inner updater satisfies"));
add(EQ("| (1/T) Σ_{t=1}^{T} err_{t} − α |  ≤  (K_{T} + 1) · (1 + (1 + n_{min}) γ) / (γ T).", 8));
add(P("*Proof.* Partition [1, T] into the K_{T} + 1 maximal segments on which α_{t} evolves by Eq. (3) without re-initialisation. On a segment [a, b], summing Eq. (3) gives Σ_{t=a}^{b}(err_{t} − α) = (α_{a} − α_{b+1})/γ. Re-initialisations set α to a value in [0, 1] (α itself, or a clipped banked level). Outside the bridge, α_{t} ≤ 0 yields q_{t} = +∞ and err_{t} = 0, so α increases; α_{t} ≥ 1 yields q_{t} = 0 and err_{t} = 1 almost surely, so α decreases. Hence α_{t} ∈ [−γ, 1 + γ] there, as in Gibbs and Candès (2021). The bridge lasts at most n_{min} steps per segment, and each step changes α by at most γ·max(α, 1 − α) ≤ γ, so it can push α at most n_{min}γ further outside this interval. Therefore |α_{a} − α_{b+1}| ≤ 1 + (1 + n_{min})γ. Summing over segments and dividing by T gives Eq. (8). □"));
add(P("**Remarks.** (i) Proposition 2 recovers the ACI bound (1 + γ)/(γT) when K_{T} = 0 and n_{min} = 0, and gives long-run coverage whenever K_{T} = o(T), for example when the number of genuine regime changes grows sublinearly (by Proposition 1, false alarms within exchangeable regimes are rare). (ii) Analogous segment-wise arguments apply to QT, whose threshold update q_{t+1} = q_{t} + η_{t}(err_{t} − α) telescopes in the same way, and to DtACI, whose guarantees (Gibbs & Candès, 2024) hold on each segment. (iii) The guarantees concern marginal, long-run coverage. Like all methods in this family, MARS-CP offers no conditional coverage guarantee, which is attainable only in restricted senses even under exchangeability (Vovk, 2013); the local behaviour after a change is an empirical question addressed in Sect. 7."));

// ================================================================ 6 SETUP
add(H1("6 Experimental Setup"));
add(P("Figure 3 summarises the experimental pipeline. Every method receives the identical score stream, so differences between methods reflect only how they construct thresholds."));
add(FIG(__dirname + "/../figures/fig3_pipeline.png", 3560, 1968, "**Fig. 3** Experimental pipeline. Real streams pass through lag and calendar features and a sliding-refit gradient-boosting forecaster; synthetic streams use a known forecaster. The resulting score stream is fed identically to the baselines and to the MARS-CP wrappers, and all methods are scored with the same metrics and statistical tests. Hyperparameters were tuned on validation seeds and then frozen"));
add(H2("6.1 Synthetic streams"));
add(P("Covariates X_{t} ∈ ℝ^{5} are standard Gaussian, the forecaster is the true regression function of the baseline regime, f̂(x) = x^{⊤}β with β = 𝟏/√5, and Y_{t} = f̂(X_{t}) + b_{t} + σ_{t}e_{t}, with T = 10,000 steps and regimes of 1,250 steps. The four scenarios are: **S1 Recurring-Abrupt**, with noise scale σ following the pattern (1, 3, 1, 0.4) repeated twice, so that it contains both harder and easier shifts that recur; **S2 Gradual**, with σ_{t} = 1 + 0.75(1 − cos(2πt/5000)), a smooth periodic drift; **S3 Stationary**, with σ = 1 throughout, used to measure false alarms; and **S4 Mixed-Recurring**, with the pattern (baseline, bias b = 2, baseline, Student-t_{2.5} heavy-tailed noise) repeated twice. Each scenario is run with 20 test seeds (0–19)."));
add(H2("6.2 Real-world streams"));
add(P("We use four public streams that exhibit different forms of non-stationarity (Table 2). For each we build lagged features, and the base forecaster is a histogram gradient-boosting regressor (Pedregosa et al., 2011) refitted every 250 steps (90 for MelbTemp) on a trailing window of 3,000 observations, and used only for data after its training window. This produces genuinely online residuals whose distribution shifts both with the data and with each refit."));
